The configuration
administrator-07-sd-admin-configuration
The page shows every parameter of sd.conf that SD knows. For each one: the value this SD is running with (what the CONFIG function reports), the value the file says now, the default, when a change takes effect, whether anything acts on the parameter, and whether this page can change it. A row marked * is one where the file and the running SD disagree, and the box at the foot says who will see the file's value. A name in the file that SD does not know is listed as unknown: SD will not start, and no new session will, while the file holds it.
session means that a session reads sd.conf itself when it starts, so an edit reaches the sessions started after it and not the ones already running. restart means the parameter sizes or starts the system and takes effect when SD is next stopped and started. D shows only the rows where the file and SD disagree, S only the parameters the file sets. B shows the saved backup directory and R the operating system part of the settings report. F5 reads everything again.
E changes the parameter under the cursor when its Change column says e: it asks for a whole number, checks it against the range SD itself enforces, shows the exact command (set.config name number) and when the change takes effect, and runs it only when you answer Run. SDSYS's elevated helper then changes that one line of C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf, keeps the file as it was as sd.conf.bak, and the page reads the file and SD again to show what changed.
Only 14 tuning parameters can be changed here. For every other row the Change column says by hand, and E shows why (the doors, paths and commands, logs and diagnostics, sizes SD does not check, and parameters nothing acts on are not changed from SD) and the line to add to sd.conf yourself, from an editor run as administrator.
